      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "\\begin{align*}\n\\text{We are finding the coefficient of the } x^2y^4\\text{ term.}\n\\text{\nThe coefficient of the } \\binom{6}{2}x^2y^4 = \\boxed{15} \\text{ term in the expansion of } (x+y)^6.\\\\\n\\end{align*} Given (x + y)^6, we need to find the coefficient of the x^2y^4 term.\n\nThe general term in the expansion of (x + y)^6 is given by:\n[\nT_{r+1} = binom{6}{r} x^{6-r} y^r\n]\nWe need the term where the power of (x) is 2 and the power of (y) is 4, which corresponds to:\n[\nr = 4\n]\nSubstituting (r = 4) into the general term, we get:\n[\nbinom{6}{4} x^{6-4} y^4 = binom{6}{4} x^2 y^4\n]\nThe coefficient of the x^2y^4 term is:\n[\nbinom{6}{4} = frac{6!}{4!(6-4)!} = frac{6!}{4! 2!}\n]\nCalculating this value, the coefficient is:\n[\nbinom{6}{4} = frac{6 times 5 times 4!}{4! times 2 times 1} = frac{6 times 5}{2 times 1} = 15\n]\nSo, the coefficient of the x^2y^4 term in the expansion is (15). The answer is (15).",
